    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Habitat of Wildlife</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain how to play a common game. Interactive: Exchange simple ideas about current events in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain the rules of a typical game related to wildlife or nature themes (e.g., "I will describe the animal. You guess."). Can describe and ask questions about current changes in animal behavior in a group discussion (e.g., "Jaguars now hunt closer to rivers because the prey moves there to find water."). Can exchange ideas about current news related to wildlife or habitats in a group discussion (e.g., "There is news about volunteers protecting sea turtle nests in Veraguas.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various Panamanian wildlife (e.g., jaguar, harpy eagle, sloth, sea turtle) and their habitats (forest, jungle, beach). Students work in pairs to identify the animals and briefly say where they live, using "There is/are" and "They live in the..." For example, "There is a jaguar. Jaguars live in the forest."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a simple game called "Guess the Animal." Teacher says, "I will describe an animal. You guess. For example, 'This animal is big. It has stripes. It hunts other animals. It lives in the jungle. What is it?' (A jaguar!) This animal moves slowly. It lives in trees. It is smaller than a jaguar. (A sloth!)" Teacher emphasizes the use of present simple for habits and comparative adjectives. Teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of long vowel sounds in 'eagle' and short vowel sounds in 'frog', and practices syllable division in 'wild-life'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of words do we use to compare two animals? (Comparative adjectives: bigger, faster)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'There are many endangered animals,' what does 'endangered' mean? (Animals that might disappear forever)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Wildlife."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue or printed text of two students discussing news about "Panama's Wildlife." The dialogue includes descriptions of animal habitats and simple current events. For example, "Hey, have you heard about the sea turtles in Veraguas? Volunteers are protecting their nests. There are more nests this year than last year!" and "Yes! That's great news. They are more resilient now."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Exchange simple ideas about current events in group discussions, using appropriate vocabulary related to wildlife and habitats.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the students talking about in the news? (Wildlife protection/sea turtles)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Volunteers are building houses for jaguars. (False)" "There are fewer sea turtle nests this year. (False)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the animal (sea turtle) with the place (Veraguas) and activity (protecting nests).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using comparative and superlative adjectives (stronger than, the fastest); "There is/there are" for descriptions; present simple for habits (moves slowly, hunts); vocabulary (habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, species, adaptation, jungle, biodiversity, protect, live, grow, hunt, survive, adapt, migrate, observe, conserve, endangered, nocturnal, wild, diverse, camouflaged, resilient). Pronunciation of long/short vowel sounds and syllable division in compound nouns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive sentences with blanks about Panamanian animals and their habitats. They complete the sentences using target vocabulary and grammar. Example: "The (jaguar) is a (predator). It (hunts) its (prey) (quickly) in the (jungle). There (are) many (species) of (wildlife) (here)." Teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of key words like 'wildlife' (wild-life) and vowel sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill - Comparative Challenge):</b> In pairs, students describe two different animals using comparative adjectives. Teacher provides sentence starters: "The [animal 1] is [comparative adjective] than the [animal 2]." For example, "The harpy eagle is stronger than the hawk." "The sloth moves slower than the monkey." "The jungle is wilder than the forest." Students practice using different animals and adjectives from the vocabulary list (e.g., bigger, smaller, faster, slower, wilder, more endangered).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Animal Facts Exchange):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student chooses two Panamanian animals (e.g., jaguar, tapir, toucan, snake). They take turns describing their chosen animals to their partner, focusing on habitat, habits (present simple), and making at least one comparative statement. Example: "My first animal lives in the forest. It is a predator. It hunts at night. It is bigger than a ocelot." The partner guesses the animal. They then discuss what they know about conserving these species.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Wildlife."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Discussion and Game Explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. First, each group creates a simple game about Panamanian wildlife (e.g., "Animal Trivia," "Habitat Matching," or a slightly more complex "Guess the Animal" version). Each group member must explain one rule or aspect of the game to the other group members, using clear sentences. For example, "First, we describe the animal's habitat. Then, you guess its species." "This game is about predators and prey. The jaguar is a predator; the sloth is prey."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After explaining their game, each group discusses a recent "wildlife news" topic presented by the teacher (e.g., "News about deforestation near a jaguar habitat," "Successful protection of an endangered bird species," "Changes in a river affecting fish and birds"). Students share simple ideas, ask questions, and use comparative adjectives. For instance, "Jaguars now hunt closer to rivers because the prey moves there to find water. This is a bigger change than last year." "There is news about volunteers protecting sea turtle nests in Veraguas. That is better for the species." The teacher facilitates, ensuring all students contribute.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students explain their group's wildlife game rules to another group and participate in the group discussion about wildlife news.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of long/short vowels, 'wildlife').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (habitat, species, protect, endangered, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of comparative adjectives and "There is/are."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining game rules.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active collaboration and exchange of ideas in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain the rules of a simple game about animals. Yes/No/A little"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I used new words like 'endangered' or 'predator' today. Yes/No"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy and what was difficult when talking about wildlife today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I practice speaking more about these topics outside of class?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities successfully engaged most students in using target vocabulary and grammar. The game explanation was a great way for them to practice productive speaking. Some students struggled with forming comparative sentences quickly; more practice with structured sentence frames might be needed. Ensure a wider range of current event topics for group discussions to keep engagement high. Pronunciation of long/short vowels and syllable division will need continued reinforcement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will use the vocabulary and grammar practiced today to write descriptive paragraphs about Panamanian wildlife and their habitats, building on their speaking practice to construct coherent written texts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>